### What is the difference between Iterator and ListIterator?

| Feature | Iterator | ListIterator |
|---|---|---|
| Works on | Any Collection | List only |
| Direction | Forward only | Forward + backward |
| hasNext() / next() | Yes | Yes |
| hasPrevious() / previous() | No | Yes |
| remove() | Yes | Yes |
| add(e) | No | Yes (insert at position) |
| set(e) | No | Yes (replace last returned) |
| nextIndex() / previousIndex() | No | Yes |
| Start at index | No (always from 0) | Yes: listIterator(index) |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class IteratorVsListIteratorDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001"));

        // Iterator: forward-only, remove-only
        System.out.println("Iterator (forward):");
        Iterator<String> it = accounts.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            System.out.println("  " + it.next());
        }

        // ListIterator: bidirectional + add + set
        System.out.println("\nListIterator (add + set):");
        ListIterator<String> lit = accounts.listIterator(1); // start before CUR3001

        // Insert before CUR3001
        lit.add("LON5001"); // [SAV2001, LON5001, CUR3001, FD4001]

        // Move forward and replace CUR3001
        String current = lit.next(); // CUR3001
        lit.set("CUR3001-UPDATED"); // replace

        // Move backward
        lit.previous(); // CUR3001-UPDATED
        lit.previous(); // LON5001

        System.out.println("Result: " + accounts);
        System.out.println("Current index: " + lit.nextIndex());
    }
}
```

```text
Iterator (forward):
  SAV2001
  CUR3001
  FD4001

ListIterator (add + set):
Result: [SAV2001, LON5001, CUR3001-UPDATED, FD4001]
Current index: 1
```

### When do you use ListIterator?

ListIterator is necessary when you need to:

- **Add elements during iteration** – only ListIterator.add() is safe; collection.add() during iteration throws CME.
- **Replace elements during iteration** – ListIterator.set() replaces the last returned element.
- **Traverse backward** – ListIterator.previous() moves in reverse.
- **Know the current position** – nextIndex() and previousIndex() give the cursor position.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class ListIteratorUseCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Scenario: insert audit markers between transactions
        List<String> transactions = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            "TXN-001: Deposit", "TXN-002: Withdraw", "TXN-003: Transfer"
        ));

        ListIterator<String> lit = transactions.listIterator();
        while (lit.hasNext()) {
            String txn = lit.next();
            lit.add("[AUDIT] Verified: " + txn); // insert after current
        }

        System.out.println("With audit markers:");
        transactions.forEach(t -> System.out.println("  " + t));
    }
}
```

```text
With audit markers:
  TXN-001: Deposit
  [AUDIT] Verified: TXN-001: Deposit
  TXN-002: Withdraw
  [AUDIT] Verified: TXN-002: Withdraw
  TXN-003: Transfer
  [AUDIT] Verified: TXN-003: Transfer
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Calling set() without calling next() or previous() first

```java
ListIterator<String> lit = list.listIterator();
// lit.set("X"); // IllegalStateException! Must call next() or previous() first.
lit.next();
lit.set("X"); // OK – replaces the element returned by next()
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **ListIterator.add() inserts before the cursor.** After add(), the cursor is between the inserted element and the next element. Calling next() returns the element after the inserted one; calling previous() returns the inserted element.

- **LinkedList + ListIterator = O(1) insert.** On LinkedList, ListIterator.add() is O(1) because it just rewires pointers. On ArrayList, it is O(n) because elements shift.

- **Spliterator is the parallel alternative.** For parallel processing, use `Spliterator` (available since Java 8) instead of Iterator.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Can Iterator move backward?" – No, only ListIterator can.
- "Can you add elements during iteration?" – Only with ListIterator.add().
- "What does set() do?" – Replaces the last element returned by next() or previous().
- "Which List benefits most from ListIterator?" – LinkedList (O(1) add/remove at position).
